{
  "id": "ep091",
  "duration": 50.495,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.929,
      "text": "A subatomic particle hit Earth with the energy of a fast-pitched baseball.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Sounding Rockets Study How Winds In Space Drive Currents in the Upper Atmosphere (5905852763).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sounding_Rockets_Study_How_Winds_In_Space_Drive_Currents_in_the_Upper_Atmosphere_(5905852763).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.933,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.929,
      "end": 12.374,
      "text": "In October 1991, the Fly's Eye detector in Utah captured a visitor traveling at nearly light speed.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.933,
          "end": 12.367,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 12.374,
      "end": 19.299,
      "text": "Dubbed the Oh-My-God particle, this single proton packed 320 exaelectronvolts of energy.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Protonshower.jpg",
        "credit": "Original uploader was Dinoj at en.wikipedia",
        "license": "CC BY 2.5",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protonshower.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 12.367,
          "end": 19.3,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 19.299,
      "end": 25.594,
      "text": "That is forty-seven million times more energy than our most powerful human-built accelerators.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "CERN OpenDays @ LHC 02.jpg",
        "credit": "SimonWaldherr",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CERN_OpenDays_@_LHC_02.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 19.3,
          "end": 25.6,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 25.594,
      "end": 31.039,
      "text": "Most people assume such force must come from a massive, visible cosmic engine.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of the active galactic nucleus of Messier 77 (eso2203d).jpg",
        "credit": "ESO/M. Kornmesser and L. Cal\u00e7ada",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_active_galactic_nucleus_of_Messier_77_(eso2203d).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 25.6,
          "end": 28.2,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 28.2,
          "end": 31.033,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 31.039,
      "end": 36.334,
      "text": "But tracing its path back reveals absolutely nothing in the darkness where it began.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "WMAP 2012.png",
        "credit": "NASA / WMAP Science Team",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WMAP_2012.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 31.033,
          "end": 33.4,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 33.4,
          "end": 36.333,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.334,
      "end": 44.004,
      "text": "In 2021, the Amaterasu particle arrived with similar power from a completely empty cosmic void.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.333,
          "end": 44.0,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 44.004,
      "end": 50.145,
      "text": "These invisible missiles prove the universe can hit us with the energy of a fast-pitched baseball.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Sounding Rockets Study How Winds In Space Drive Currents in the Upper Atmosphere (5905852763).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sounding_Rockets_Study_How_Winds_In_Space_Drive_Currents_in_the_Upper_Atmosphere_(5905852763).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 44.0,
          "end": 50.5,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
